.Dd May 18, 2026
.Dt PWDB 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pwdb_die ,
.Nm pwdb_valid_name ,
.Nm pwdb_lock ,
.Nm pwdb_unlock ,
.Nm pwdb_atomic_rewrite ,
.Nm pwdb_next_free_id ,
.Nm pwdb_today_days ,
.Nm pwdb_split ,
.Nm pwdb_require_root
.Nd passwd / group database helpers
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libpwdb
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/pwdb.h
.Ft void
.Fn pwdb_die "const char *progname" "const char *fmt" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn pwdb_valid_name "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn pwdb_lock "void"
.Ft void
.Fn pwdb_unlock "int fd"
.Ft typedef int (*pwdb_write_cb)(FILE *out, void *arg);
.Ft int
.Fn pwdb_atomic_rewrite "const char *path" "mode_t default_mode" "pwdb_write_cb cb" "void *arg"
.Ft long
.Fn pwdb_next_free_id "int is_group" "long min" "long max"
.Ft long
.Fn pwdb_today_days "void"
.Ft int
.Fn pwdb_split "char *line" "char delim" "char **out" "int out_max"
.Ft void
.Fn pwdb_require_root "const char *progname"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Lb libpwdb
collects the shared plumbing every substrate user/group admin tool
needs: an atomic-rewrite helper that won't strand a half-edited
.Pa /etc/passwd
on a crash, a cross-tool lock, a portable name validator, and a
.Vt UID Ns / Ns Vt GID
allocator that walks the existing database.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_die
prints
.Dq Fa progname : Fa fmt No \\n
to
.Dv stderr
and calls
.Xr exit 3
with status 1.  Convenience for fatal-on-argv-error paths.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_valid_name
returns nonzero when
.Fa name
is a valid POSIX-portable account name: first character alphabetic
or underscore, body
.Bq Ar a-z0-9_- ,
length 1..32.  Zero on any failure.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_lock
opens
.Pa /etc/.pwd.lock
.Pq creating it 0600 if needed
and takes an exclusive
.Xr fcntl 2
.Dv F_SETLKW
advisory lock spanning the whole file.  Returns the open file
descriptor (which the caller passes to
.Fn pwdb_unlock )
or -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Fn pwdb_unlock
releases the lock and closes the descriptor.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_atomic_rewrite
implements the rename-after-fsync idiom every entry in this library
expects callers to use for database edits:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Open
.Fa path Ns .new
with
.Dv O_CREAT|O_EXCL
\(em fails loudly on a leftover from a previous crash.
.It
Call
.Fa cb
with the new file's
.Vt FILE * ;
.Fa cb
fills it and returns 0, or returns -1 to abort.
.It
.Fn fflush ,
.Fn fsync ,
.Fn fclose .
.It
Preserve owner/mode from the existing
.Fa path
where it exists, else use
.Fa default_mode .
.It
.Xr rename 2
the temp file over
.Fa path .
.El
On failure the
.Fa path Ns .new
file is unlinked.  Returns 0 on success, -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_next_free_id
scans the passwd database (
.Fa is_group No == 0
) or the group database (
.Fa is_group No != 0
) and returns the lowest unused id within
.Bq Fa min , Fa max ,
or -1 when the range is exhausted.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_today_days
returns days since
.Dq 1970-01-01 UTC \(em the unit the
.Pa /etc/shadow
lastchange / inactive / expire fields use.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_split
splits
.Fa line
in place on
.Fa delim ,
writing up to
.Fa out_max
NUL-terminated field pointers into
.Fa out .
A trailing
.Dq \\n
on the last field is stripped.
Returns the number of fields actually produced.
.Pp
.Fn pwdb_require_root
calls
.Fn pwdb_die
(and exits) when
.Xr geteuid 2
is non-zero.  All write-side admin tools call this immediately after
parsing arguments.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
See the individual function paragraphs above.  As a class:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn pwdb_lock
.It Fn pwdb_atomic_rewrite
.It Fn pwdb_next_free_id
return -1 on failure with
.Va errno
set.
.It Fn pwdb_valid_name
returns 0 (invalid) or non-zero (valid).
.It Fn pwdb_die
.It Fn pwdb_require_root
do not return on the error path \(em they call
.Xr exit 3 .
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn pwdb_lock
and
.Fn pwdb_atomic_rewrite
can fail with any
.Va errno
returned by
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
or
.Xr chown 2 .
Notable values:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Bq Er EEXIST
.Fa path Ns .new
already exists \(em leftover from a previous crash, clean it up
before retrying.
.It Bq Er EACCES
The caller lacks write permission on the database file or its
directory.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
.Fa path
is too long to derive a sibling
.Fa path Ns .new .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A minimal "remove user from /etc/passwd" tool:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <sys/pwdb.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

struct ctx { const char *user; };

static int
strip_user(FILE *out, void *arg)
{
    struct ctx *c = arg;
    FILE *in = fopen(PWDB_PASSWD, "r");
    char  line[1024];
    size_t nlen = strlen(c->user);
    if (!in) return -1;
    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, in))
        if (strncmp(line, c->user, nlen) != 0 || line[nlen] != ':')
            fputs(line, out);
    fclose(in);
    return 0;
}

int
main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    pwdb_require_root("rmuser");
    int lk = pwdb_lock();
    struct ctx c = { .user = argv[1] };
    int rc = pwdb_atomic_rewrite(PWDB_PASSWD, 0644, strip_user, &c);
    pwdb_unlock(lk);
    return rc == 0 ? 0 : 1;
}
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Pa /etc/.pwd.lock
.It Pa /etc/passwd
The account database.
.It Pa /etc/group
The group database.
.It Pa /etc/shadow
The shadow password database.
.It Pa /etc/gshadow
The shadow group-password database.
.It Pa /etc/.pwd.lock
Cross-tool exclusive lock.
.El
